QA CI/CD rule violated — *"Make test failures visible and actionable in CI output"*; a suite that disables itself based on the module under test is not actionable.

`gitAvailable()` is imported from `../src/packs/sync.js` — the module these 7 `{ skip }` tests exercise — and is also the production guard that makes `packsSync` print "git is not on PATH" and return 1. If `gitAvailable()` regresses to returning `false` (a changed probe command, a swallowed spawn error), every clone/pull/safety test in this file turns into a silent skip and `npm run test` still reports green, while the shipped `packs sync` refuses to run for every user. Nothing in the suite asserts `gitAvailable()` is ever `true`.

Fix — gate on an independent probe and assert the function under test agrees with it:

```ts
let HAVE_GIT = true;
try { execFileSync('git', ['--version'], { stdio: 'ignore' }); } catch { HAVE_GIT = false; }
const skip = HAVE_GIT ? false : 'git not on PATH';

test('gitAvailable — reports true when git really is on PATH', { skip }, () => {
  assert.equal(gitAvailable(), true);
});
```

QA CI/CD rule violated — *"Make test failures visible and actionable in CI output — include test name, failure reason"*.

`readdirSync(FIXTURES)` returns every entry, not just directories, and `runCase` is called outside any try/catch. Two concrete consequences:

1. A non-directory in `evals/fixtures/` (a `README.md` describing the fixture format, a stray `.gitkeep`) makes `readFileSync(join(dir, 'diff.patch'))` throw ENOENT, which escapes the loop and aborts the whole harness — the cases that already ran lose their summary line and the ones queued after never execute.
2. A typo'd case name (`node scripts/eval.mjs sql-injecton`) produces a raw ENOENT stack trace rather than "unknown case".

Fix — list only directories and turn a per-case throw into a per-case failure:

```js
const cases = caseName ? [caseName] : readdirSync(FIXTURES, { withFileTypes: true }).filter((d) => d.isDirectory()).map((d) => d.name);
for (const c of cases) {
  if (!existsSync(join(FIXTURES, c, 'diff.patch'))) { console.error(`✗ ${c}: no such fixture`); failures++; continue; }
  try { if (!runCase(c, extra)) failures++; }
  catch (err) { console.error(`✗ ${c}: ${err.message}`); failures++; }
}
```

`loadSkillFile(join(dir, rel))` is called with no error handling, so any read failure propagates out of `loadPackSkills` → `loadAll` → `runReview` and kills the whole run. This contradicts the fail-soft contract the module itself documents two lines above ("A missing checkout simply contributes nothing") and the one `ensurePacks` states ("Every failure is a warning, never fatal — a review must run with whatever packs are on disk").

The read is not hypothetical-only: `listPackFiles` uses `readdirSync(dir, { recursive: true })` without `withFileTypes`, so *directories* whose name ends in `.md` (or a symlink to one that resolves inside the pack) survive every filter and hit `readFileSync` as EISDIR; and packs are a shared, mutable cache — a `pr-review packs sync` (or an auto-clone from a second concurrent review) that rewrites the checkout between the `listPackFiles` enumeration and this read yields ENOENT. Either way an unexpected error escapes with no handler at the level that can absorb it.

Rule (OWASP Error Handling — the handler must turn an unexpected failure into a controlled outcome, logged for investigation rather than propagated): wrap the per-file load and skip that file:

```ts
let def;
try {
  def = loadSkillFile(join(dir, rel));
} catch (err) {
  process.stderr.write(`[packs] warning: ${printable(pack.name)}: could not read ${printable(rel)} — skipped (${printable((err as Error).message.split('\n')[0] ?? '')})\n`);
  continue;
}
```

Also filter to regular files in `listPackFiles` (`readdirSync(dir, { recursive: true, withFileTypes: true })` + `e.isFile()`) so a `.md` directory never reaches the read.

**Rule (OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet — "Data to exclude"):** access tokens and connection strings "should usually not be recorded directly in the logs, but instead should be removed, masked, sanitized, hashed, or encrypted."

`origin` here is the raw output of `git remote get-url origin`, which routinely carries userinfo credentials: GitLab CI writes `https://gitlab-ci-token:<job-token>@host/...` into `.git/config`, and manually-set PAT remotes (`https://<PAT>@github.com/...`) are common on Windows. This note is then written to three places:

- `src/commands/review.ts:642` — `process.stderr.write('[stack] ' + n)`, and detached runs (the default path the slash command uses) redirect stderr into the durable `~/.pr-review/runs/<id>/detached.log` (`src/commands/detach.ts:41-45`)
- `src/commands/review.ts:694` — `lines.push('- _' + n + '_')` on `--context-only`, persisted into `pr-review-summary.md`

So the token lands in a shareable on-disk artifact, and precisely in the case this branch fires (cwd is *not* the PR's repo — the normal CI/multi-repo situation).

**Fix:** mask userinfo before it reaches the note. Strip it where the remote is read, so every consumer benefits:

```ts
function redactUrl(u: string): string {
  return u.replace(/\/\/[^@/]+@/, '//***@');
}
// ...
notes.push(
  `cwd is not a checkout of ${opts.pr.owner}/${opts.pr.repo} (origin: ${origin ? redactUrl(origin) : 'none'}) — dependency tags skipped`,
);
```

**Rule (OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet — "Event collection"):** "Perform sanitization on all event data to prevent log injection attacks e.g. carriage return (CR), line feed (LF) and delimiter characters."

`firstLine()` splits on `\n` only. A `\r`, an ANSI escape (`\u001b`), or any other C0 control character survives into the returned string, which is then interpolated into warnings at `src/packs/sync.ts:206` and `src/packs/sync.ts:260` and written straight out — `process.stderr.write(w + '\n')` (`src/commands/review.ts:628`), `console.error(w)` (`src/commands/packs.ts:98`), `console.error(f.error)` (`src/commands/packs.ts:59`) — and thence into the persistent `detached.log`. The source text is a third-party git remote's stderr (`remote:` side-band messages are echoed verbatim by git), so a hostile or compromised pack host can overwrite the visible log line with a bare CR or inject terminal escapes.

This PR already introduces `printable()` in `src/plugins/builtin.ts` for exactly this purpose and applies it to pack-derived strings in `src/plugins/builtin.ts` and `src/packs/load.ts` — `sync.ts` is the one path that skips it.

**Fix:** strip control characters, not just the newline:

```ts
import { printable } from '../plugins/builtin.js';

function firstLine(err: unknown): string {
  const e = err as Error & { stderr?: string };
  const text = (e.stderr ?? e.message ?? String(err)).toString().trim();
  return printable(text.split(/\r?\n/)[0] ?? '') || 'unknown error';
}
```

`firstLine()` returns the **first** line of `err.stderr`, but git writes its progress banner there before any error. Verified locally against a non-existent repo:

```
Cloning into 'C:/.../nopeclone'...
remote: Repository not found.
fatal: repository 'https://github.com/.../nope.git/' not found
```

`firstLine()` returns `Cloning into 'C:/.../nopeclone'...` — the real cause is discarded. Both consumers surface that string as the whole diagnosis:

- `ensurePacks` (line 206): `[packs] owasp: clone failed — Cloning into 'C:\Users\...\packs\owasp'...; continuing without it`
- `syncPacks` (line 260): `✗ owasp — Cloning into '...'... (if upstream force-pushed, delete ... and re-sync)`

Repo-not-found, auth failure, DNS/proxy failure, and the 5-minute `GIT_TIMEOUT_MS` kill all render identically and none of them actionably. Since a pack that fails to clone silently removes review passes from every subsequent run, this message is the user's only signal — and it says nothing.

Fix: pick the meaningful line, not the first one:

```ts
function firstLine(err: unknown): string {
  const e = err as Error & { stderr?: string };
  const text = (e.stderr ?? e.message ?? String(err)).toString().trim();
  const lines = text.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  return (
    lines.filter((l) => /^(fatal|error|remote):/i.test(l)).pop() ??
    lines.find((l) => !/^Cloning into /i.test(l)) ??
    lines[0] ??
    'unknown error'
  );
}
```

With `force: true`, `loadLinguist` skips the cache-first branch, attempts the download, and on failure hits:

```ts
catch (err) {
  if (existsSync(cache)) { const cached = readCache(); if (cached) return cached; }
  ...warn...; return null;
}
```

A user who already has a cache therefore gets a **non-null** return from a refresh that failed — and the stderr warning is only in the `return null` branch, so nothing at all is printed. This line then reports `✓ Linguist languages.yml refreshed`.

Failure scenario: offline / proxy-blocked / GitHub 5xx. The user runs `pr-review packs sync` precisely to refresh review knowledge, sees `✓` for Linguist, and keeps reviewing with month-old language data — believing it is current. There is no way to discover this short of stat-ing the cache file by hand.

Secondary: the doc comment on `packsSync` says "Exit 1 on any failure", but `return result.failed.length > 0 ? 1 : 0` ignores the Linguist outcome entirely, so a genuinely failed refresh (`null`) still exits 0 and CI wrappers see success.

Fix: have `loadLinguist` report *how* it resolved (e.g. return `{ index, refreshed: boolean, error?: string }`, or accept a `force` mode that does not fall back), print the real state, and fold a failed forced refresh into the exit code.

`readOrEmpty` swallows every parse error and returns `{}`:

```ts
try { return (parseYaml(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')) as GlobalRawConfig) ?? {}; }
catch { return {}; }
```

`packsAdd` treats that empty object as "nothing configured", materializes `DEFAULT_PACKS`, appends the new spec, and calls `writeConfig(cfg, path)` — which **overwrites the whole file** with only `skill_packs`. Every other key the user had (`default_model`, `hosts`, `extra_skills_dirs`, `skip_reviewers`, `dedupe`, `invoke_codex`, …) is gone, with no backup and no error.

Failure scenario: `~/.pr-review/config.yaml` has one bad indent (or a tab, the classic YAML trap). `pr-review packs add owner/repo` prints `(skill_packs was not set — materialized the default pack list into the config first)` — which is also **false**, since `skill_packs` may well have been set — followed by `added owner/repo to ...`. Both messages read as success while the user's configuration is being deleted.

This is a read-modify-write against a user-owned file; a failed read must abort, never be treated as an empty file. Fix:

```ts
// configure.ts
export function readOrEmpty(path = GLOBAL_PATH): GlobalRawConfig {
  if (!existsSync(path)) return {};
  try { return (parseYaml(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')) as GlobalRawConfig) ?? {}; }
  catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`${path} is not valid YAML (${(err as Error).message.split('\n')[0]}) — fix it before editing it with pr-review; refusing to overwrite it`);
  }
}
```

Note also that even the success path silently strips every comment from the user's config (acknowledged in the `readOrEmpty` doc comment but never surfaced to the user) — worth a one-line notice on write.

The hint is appended unconditionally to whatever `firstLine(err)` produced, so it lands on failures it does not describe:

- a clone failure (the `!existsSync(dir)` branch) — the hint tells the user to delete a directory that was never created;
- a network/DNS/proxy failure or the 5-minute `GIT_TIMEOUT_MS` kill — deleting a good, fully-synced checkout is the *worst* response, and on the next run the pack has to re-clone over the same broken network;
- a permission error on `pull`.

Combined with the `firstLine()` bug above, the user sees `✗ owasp — Cloning into '...'... (if upstream force-pushed, delete C:\Users\...\packs\owasp and re-sync)` for a transient DNS blip, and following that advice throws away working review knowledge.

Fix: condition the hint on the error actually indicating a diverged history, e.g.

```ts
const msg = firstLine(err);
const diverged = /non-fast-forward|not possible to fast-forward|diverged|refusing to merge unrelated histories/i.test(String((err as Error & { stderr?: string }).stderr ?? msg));
result.failed.push({ name: pack.name, error: diverged ? `${msg} (upstream history changed — delete ${dir} and re-sync)` : msg });
```

`writeFileSync(packMetaPath(...))` failing is caught and ignored with `// best-effort`, but this metadata is not decorative — it is the **only** record of `syncedAt`, and `packStatus`/`ensurePacks`/`doctor`/`packs list` all derive staleness from it (`ageDays === null` counts as stale).

Failure scenario: `~/.pr-review/packs/` is read-only, on a full disk, or the `.json` is locked by an AV scanner. `pr-review packs sync` prints `✓ owasp @ a1b2c3d` and exits 0, because `syncPacks` pushes to `result.synced` regardless of whether the meta write landed. The very next review prints `[packs] owasp: last sync unknown age — run \`pr-review packs sync\` to refresh review knowledge`, and `doctor` reports `never synced`. The user runs sync again, sees `✓`, and gets the same warning forever with nothing anywhere naming the cause (EACCES/ENOSPC).

Same class of hidden error: EROFS, EPERM, ENOSPC, EBUSY — none of which the user can guess.

Fix: surface it and let the caller decide, rather than reporting success:

```ts
try {
  writeFileSync(packMetaPath(pack, home), JSON.stringify(meta, null, 2), 'utf8');
} catch (err) {
  throw new Error(`synced but could not record sync metadata at ${packMetaPath(pack, home)}: ${(err as Error).message} — the pack will keep reporting as stale`);
}
```

(or return a `metaWritten: false` flag and have `packsSync` print `⚠` instead of `✓`).

`readdirSync` failing returns `[]`, and `loadPackSkills`'s doc comment justifies the silence with *"A missing checkout simply contributes nothing (ensurePacks already warned)"*. But `loadPackSkills` only calls `listPackFiles` **after** `existsSync(dir)` passes, so the missing-checkout case never reaches here. What reaches here is the case nobody warned about: a directory that exists but cannot be enumerated.

Hidden errors: EACCES/EPERM (a pack cloned under a different user, or a locked-down `%USERPROFILE%`), EMFILE/ENFILE (file-descriptor exhaustion — plausible given `recursive: true` over 600+ files across three packs), ENOTDIR, EIO on a network home dir, and on Windows a transient sharing violation while an AV scanner walks the fresh clone.

User impact: the pack is reported present by `ensurePacks`, `packs list` shows `✓ owasp — 0 skill(s)`, and the review runs with the baselines from that pack silently absent. Exit code 0, summary looks normal.

Fix: warn with the real error and the pack path, since the caller cannot distinguish `[]` from "empty pack":

```ts
} catch (err) {
  process.stderr.write(`[packs] warning: cannot read ${printable(dir)} (${(err as Error).message}) — 0 skills loaded from this pack\n`);
  return [];
}
```

`loadSkillFile` does an unguarded `readFileSync` and is called in a loop over every file `listPackFiles` returned — hundreds of files from repos this project does not control (`github/awesome-copilot` alone loads ~600). There is no try/catch here and none in `loadAll`/`runReview` above it, so a single failure kills the run.

Hidden errors: EACCES on one file, EBUSY/EPERM while `git` or an AV scanner holds a handle mid-clone, EISDIR if upstream ships a directory named `something.md`, ENOENT on a race with a concurrent `packs sync`, and `RangeError: Invalid string length` on a pathologically large file. The user sees an unhandled `Error: EBUSY: resource busy or locked, open 'C:\Users\...\packs\awesome-copilot\instructions\foo.instructions.md'` with no indication that a *skill pack* caused it, no pack name, and no suggestion (`packs sync`, delete the pack dir).

The blast radius is wrong: one bad third-party file should cost one skill, not the whole review — which is exactly the contract `ensurePacks` already honours ("a review must run with whatever packs are on disk").

Fix:

```ts
let def;
try {
  def = loadSkillFile(join(dir, rel));
} catch (err) {
  process.stderr.write(`[packs] warning: ${pack.name}: could not load ${printable(rel)} (${(err as Error).message}) — skill skipped\n`);
  continue;
}
```

The block above this correctly guards `skill_packs` not being an array ("a scalar is a typo — neither may crash every CLI command"), but the same reasoning is not applied to the entry fields:

- `include: 'cheatsheets/*.md'` (string instead of a list — the single most likely yaml mistake) passes `obj.include && obj.include.length > 0` because strings have `.length`, and is stored as `string[]`. It then reaches `matchesAny(rel, include)` → `patterns.some is not a function`, thrown from `listPackFiles` **outside** its try/catch, killing the review with a `TypeError` that never mentions config, the key, or the file it came from.
- `baseline: 'error-handling'` produces the same crash via `p.baseline.map(...)` in `review.ts`.
- `exclude: 'hunting-*'` silently excludes nothing (`matchesAny` is never reached because the `length > 0` guard passes but the `.some` call throws) — or, for an empty string, silently disables exclusion.
- `mode: 'indx'` (or `Index`, or `true`) silently becomes `'auto'` via `obj.mode === 'index' ? 'index' : 'auto'`. A pack the user deliberately marked index-only then starts dispatching its skills as review passes — third-party prompt content promoted into the review by a typo, with no warning.

Fix: coerce and validate each field, warning on anything unexpected:

```ts
const asList = (v: unknown, key: string): string[] => {
  if (v === undefined) return [];
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return v.map(String);
  process.stderr.write(`[config] warning: skill_packs[${inferredName}].${key} must be a list — ignoring it\n`);
  return [];
};
if (obj.mode !== undefined && obj.mode !== 'index' && obj.mode !== 'auto') {
  process.stderr.write(`[config] warning: skill_packs[${inferredName}].mode '${String(obj.mode)}' is not 'auto' or 'index' — defaulting to 'auto'\n`);
}
```

`afterTriage.slice(0, MAX_TOTAL_PASSES)` silently discards anything past 16. Every other budget in this file warns loudly when it bites — `renderPassFile`, `renderProjectFile`, and `renderUnionFile` all `process.stderr.write` on truncation, and the header comment states "Truncation always warns". This one does not, and the comment ("the selection module already caps, but this file owns the token budget") explains why the guard exists but not why it may fire silently.

Failure scenario: a user configures a pack with 15 `baseline:` entries. `selectPasses` deliberately dispatches **every** baseline ("Baselines ALWAYS dispatch — they are the generic lenses of every code review"), producing 6 stack + 15 baseline = 21 passes. Five explicitly-configured baselines are then dropped here and re-labelled `matchedBy: 'index'` in `passes.json`, so the summary reports them as on-demand index entries rather than as dropped — actively misreporting the reason. Nothing distinguishes "never matched" from "matched, guaranteed to run, then trimmed for budget".

Fix:

```ts
if (capOverflow.length > 0) {
  process.stderr.write(
    `[single-session] warning: ${capOverflow.length} pass(es) exceed the ${MAX_TOTAL_PASSES}-pass budget and were moved to the on-demand index: ${capOverflow.map((p) => p.name).join(', ')}\n`,
  );
}
```

`expected.must_find ?? []` means a fixture whose `expected.yaml` has a typo (`mustFind:`, `must-find:`), a wrong indent that nests the key, or no key at all iterates zero regexes, sets `ok = true`, deletes the run dir, and reports the case as passing. `scripts/eval.mjs` then prints `all 3 eval case(s) passed`.

That is the worst place for a silent pass: this harness is the project's stated regression net ("A real production miss becomes an eval fixture… then prove the pipeline catches it"). A fixture that asserts nothing looks exactly like a fixture that caught everything, so a genuine pipeline regression can ship green.

Related in the same function: `runCase` is not wrapped, so a stray non-directory under `evals/fixtures/` or a fixture missing `diff.patch` throws and kills the whole harness mid-run instead of failing one case; and an invalid regex in `must_find` throws uncaught from `new RegExp`.

Fix:

```js
const patterns = expected?.must_find;
if (!Array.isArray(patterns) || patterns.length === 0) {
  console.error(`  ✗ ${name}: expected.yaml has no non-empty must_find list — fixture asserts nothing`);
  return false;
}
```

The comment justifies swallowing the `rmSync` failure with *"leave it; the not-a-git-checkout warning will fire next run"*, but that is only true when the leftover has no `.git`. The case this cleanup exists for — the `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` on line 192 throwing because the clone left a `.git` directory with no commit — leaves a directory where `isGitCheckout(dir)` returns **true**.

So on the next run `ensurePacks` takes the `existsSync(dir)` branch, skips the not-a-git-checkout warning, pushes the pack into `present`, and only emits the generic staleness line (`last sync unknown age`). `loadPackSkills` then loads whatever partial file set survived — commonly zero skills — and the review runs without that pack's passes, permanently, until someone deletes the directory by hand. Nothing ever says the checkout is broken.

This is not exotic on Windows (the platform this project is developed on): `EBUSY`/`EPERM` on `rmSync` of a fresh `.git` while git or an AV scanner still holds handles is routine.

Fix: report the failed cleanup so the state is visible and self-correcting, e.g.

```ts
} catch (rmErr) {
  warnings.push(`[packs] ${pack.name}: could not remove the partial checkout at ${dir} (${(rmErr as Error).message}) — delete it manually before the next sync`);
}
```

and make the `existsSync(dir)` branch verify `HEAD` resolves, not just that `.git` exists.

`syncPacks` guards a changed `ref` ("pull keeps tracking the branch the clone was made from — a config ref change silently would not take effect") but applies no equivalent guard to `git`, even though `PackMeta.git` is written on every sync precisely to record which repo the checkout came from.

Failure scenario: a user edits `skill_packs` to point the same `name:` at a different repo (forking a pack, or moving from `OWASP/CheatSheetSeries` to an internal mirror). `packDir` is keyed on `name`, so the old checkout is found, `isGitCheckout` passes, `prior.ref === pack.ref`, and the code runs `git pull --ff-only` against the **old** origin. `writeMeta` then rewrites the metadata with `git: pack.git` — the new URL. From that point `packs list` and `doctor` report the new source while every review pass is fed the old repo's content, and no re-sync can ever fix it because the metadata now looks correct. The stale review knowledge is invisible.

Fix: mirror the ref guard right next to it —
```ts
if (prior && prior.git !== pack.git) {
  result.failed.push({ name: pack.name, error: `configured git source changed (${prior.git} → ${pack.git}) — delete ${dir} and re-sync` });
  continue;
}
```
